  "preview": "A preschool has 72 ft of plastic edging for a new sandbox. Should they build it circular, square, or hexagonal? Which gives the kids the most sand to play in?",
  "body": {
    "goal": "Compare areas of regular polygons and circles with the same perimeter (the isoperimetric idea, in disguise). Move flexibly between perimeter, side length, radius, and area, and notice that for a fixed perimeter, rounder shapes enclose more area.",
    "studentPrompt": "The Sunny Days Preschool just bought 72 feet of soft plastic edging to build a new sandbox in their play yard. The director is trying to decide on the shape. Use 3.14 for π.\n\n1. If they build a SQUARE sandbox using all 72 ft of edging, what is its area?\n2. If they build a CIRCULAR sandbox using all 72 ft of edging, what is its area? (Hint: 72 ft is the circumference.)\n3. What is the difference in area between the circular sandbox and the square sandbox?\n4. The director's assistant suggests a REGULAR HEXAGON instead. Without computing the exact area, predict: will the hexagonal sandbox give more or less play area than the square? Than the circle? Explain your reasoning.\n5. Now check your prediction. The area of a regular hexagon with side length s is approximately 2.598 × s². Find the area of the hexagonal sandbox and compare.",
    "image": {
      "alt": "Three sandbox shapes drawn at the same scale on a grassy play yard: a square outlined in tan, a circle outlined in tan, and a regular hexagon outlined in tan, each filled with a light sandy color.",
      "svg": "<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 480 220\" font-family=\"system-ui, sans-serif\"><defs><linearGradient id=\"grass\" x1=\"0\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"0\" y2=\"1\"><stop offset=\"0\" stop-color=\"#cfe8b9\"/><stop offset=\"1\" stop-color=\"#9fc977\"/></linearGradient><pattern id=\"sand\" patternUnits=\"userSpaceOnUse\" width=\"6\" height=\"6\"><rect width=\"6\" height=\"6\" fill=\"#f3e1b1\"/><circle cx=\"1.5\" cy=\"1.5\" r=\"0.5\" fill=\"#d9bf7a\"/><circle cx=\"4\" cy=\"4\" r=\"0.5\" fill=\"#d9bf7a\"/></pattern></defs><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"480\" height=\"220\" fill=\"url(#grass)\"/><g><rect x=\"30\" y=\"75\" width=\"90\" height=\"90\" fill=\"url(#sand)\" stroke=\"#a8742a\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><text x=\"75\" y=\"195\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#1f2a44\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\">Square</text></g><g><circle cx=\"240\" cy=\"120\" r=\"50\" fill=\"url(#sand)\" stroke=\"#a8742a\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><text x=\"240\" y=\"195\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#1f2a44\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\">Circle</text></g><g><polygon points=\"400,75 440,95 440,140 400,160 360,140 360,95\" fill=\"url(#sand)\" stroke=\"#a8742a\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><text x=\"400\" y=\"195\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#1f2a44\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\">Hexagon</text></g><text x=\"240\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#1f2a44\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">72 ft of edging — which shape holds the most sand?</text></svg>"
    },
    "launch": "Show a quick photo or sketch of a backyard sandbox. Ask: 'If you had a long piece of edging and you got to choose the shape of your sandbox, what would you pick?' Most will say square or rectangle. Let the question 'does the shape matter?' hang for a second, then send them to the boards with the 72 ft constraint.",
    "materials": [
      "Vertical whiteboards",
      "Markers",
      "Calculators (optional — encourage estimation first)"
    ],
    "concepts": [
      "circumference",
      "area-of-circles",
      "area-of-polygons",
      "perimeter-vs-area",
      "isoperimetric-comparison"
    ],
    "taskType": "investigation",
    "btcFit": "Strong BTC task. Low floor (Q1 is pure square area). Q2 forces students to back out the radius from circumference — that's where productive struggle starts. Q4 is the diamond: it asks for reasoning before computation, which is exactly the BTC move.",
    "anticipatedApproaches": "- Q1: Side = 72/4 = 18 ft. Area = 18² = 324 sq ft.\n- Q2: Students set 2πr = 72, solve r = 72/(2·3.14) ≈ 11.46 ft. Area = π·r² ≈ 3.14·(11.46)² ≈ 412.5 sq ft. Some students will get stuck trying to use the diameter as 72 — circulate and ask 'what does the 72 measure on this shape?'\n- Q3: ≈ 412.5 − 324 ≈ 88.5 sq ft more in the circle.\n- Q4: Strong groups reason 'the more sides a regular polygon has, the rounder it is, so the closer it gets to a circle. The hexagon should be between the square and the circle.'\n- Q5: Side = 72/6 = 12 ft. Area ≈ 2.598·144 ≈ 374 sq ft. Confirms the prediction: square < hexagon < circle.",
    "commonMisconceptions": "- Treating 72 ft as the diameter of the circle instead of the circumference.\n- Confusing perimeter and area: 'all three shapes have the same perimeter, so they have the same area.'\n- For the hexagon, dividing 72 by 4 (a square habit) instead of 6.\n- Believing the square 'must' be biggest because it 'fits the most' — a guess based on familiarity, not math.",
    "discussionQuestions": "- Two shapes had the same amount of edging but different amounts of sand inside. How is that possible?\n- What pattern do you notice as the shape gets 'rounder' — more sides, then a circle?\n- If we could build a regular 100-gon with 72 ft of edging, what would you expect its area to be? Why?\n- A rectangle is NOT a regular shape. Could a long skinny rectangle with perimeter 72 ft have MORE area than the square? Less? Try one.",
    "extensions": "- A long thin rectangle: 35 ft by 1 ft has perimeter 72 ft. What is its area? Now try 30 by 6, 25 by 11, 20 by 16, 18 by 18. Graph (one side) vs (area). What shape rectangle gives the maximum area for a fixed perimeter? Connect to the square answer.\n- Reverse the question: the director decides she wants exactly 400 sq ft of sandbox area. How much edging would each shape require? Rank from least to most edging.\n- The preschool decides to use the edging to make TWO separate sandboxes (a big one and a small one), both circles, splitting the 72 ft. Is the total area larger or smaller than one big circle with all 72 ft? Why?",
    "sampleSolutions": "1. Side = 72 ÷ 4 = 18 ft. Area = 18 × 18 = 324 sq ft.\n2. Circumference 2πr = 72, so r = 72 ÷ (2 × 3.14) = 72 ÷ 6.28 ≈ 11.46 ft. Area = π·r² ≈ 3.14 × 11.46² ≈ 3.14 × 131.3 ≈ 412.5 sq ft.\n3. Difference ≈ 412.5 − 324 ≈ 88.5 sq ft. The circle gives about 88 more square feet of play area with the same edging.\n4. Prediction: hexagon should be between square and circle, because more sides = closer to a circle. Both more than the square and less than the circle.\n5. Side = 72 ÷ 6 = 12 ft. Area ≈ 2.598 × 12² = 2.598 × 144 ≈ 374 sq ft. Confirms: 324 (square) < 374 (hexagon) < 412.5 (circle). For a fixed perimeter, the circle wins.",
